      "id": "6drcsud",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.1 Basic Computer Organisation"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Basic Computer Organisation defines the functional units including CPU, Memory, and Input/Output components.",
        "The Instruction Execution Cycle consists of Fetch, Decode, Execute, and Writeback stages.",
        "Memory Hierarchy organizes memory based on speed, cost, and capacity from Registers down to Secondary Storage.",
        "Number Systems and Conversions cover Binary, Octal, Decimal, Hexadecimal, and Binary Arithmetic representations.",
        "Pipelining optimizes execution throughput by overlapping multiple instruction processing stages.",
        "RISC emphasizes simple, fixed-length instructions, whereas CISC utilizes complex, variable-length instruction sets."
      ],
      "id": "kc0m84w"
    },
